WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.280
How did Logitech become so dominant?

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:08.800
This company rules the Mice industry.&nbsp;
Be it business, personal, or gaming.&nbsp;

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:13.800
They’re the most dominant, by far.
A $15 billion company.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:18.880
Almost 5 billion in revenue.
One of the most iconic mice in history.&nbsp;

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:23.680
Ironically, the founders didn’t&nbsp;
even want to produce mice.&nbsp;

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:26.160
So, how exactly?
How did they replace&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:32.200
Razer in gaming? When most of their mice&nbsp;
look so normal, ordinary, even boring?&nbsp;

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:39.560
This is how Logitech became the new Razer.
And why it might be at risk of losing it all.

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:49.840
One of the weirdest parts to all this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:54.560
is that Logitech was never even meant&nbsp;
to be a mouse or hardware company.

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:58.680
[Pierluigi Zappacosta, Co-Founder]:"We&nbsp;
didn't want to be in mice. They seemed to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:04.240
beneath our intelligence. We wanted to&nbsp;
be a software company—like Microsoft."&nbsp;

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:09.720
The name itself is "logiciel", which is&nbsp;
literally French for software, plus "tech”.&nbsp;

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:15.320
The founders, Daniel Borel, and Pierluigi&nbsp;
Zappacosta, were Stanford students, but were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:20.880
originally from Switzerland and Italy, and they&nbsp;
were inspired by the exploding Silicon Valley.&nbsp;

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:25.280
“The partners hoped to bring the same sense of&nbsp;
entrepreneurship that they had found in California&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:31.120
to the European computer industry.”
But there was a problem: Backing.&nbsp;

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:35.640
Despite the explosion of software companies&nbsp;
in the US, finding funding in Europe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:43.156
was still “fixed on an older corporate model.”&nbsp;
Software was probably a bit too new and risky.&nbsp;

00:01:43.156 --> 00:01:48.920
And in 1984, their first big break came after&nbsp;
they won a contract from HP to supply mice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:53.880
as well as other computer peripherals.
The P4. Wow.&nbsp;

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:59.520
But this put Logitech on the map.
Then, came deals with AT&amp;T, Olivetti,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:04.600
Convergent Technologies, and DEC.
Then came IBM, by far the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:10.320
biggest player in personal computing.
That year, Logitech had sold 25,000 mice.&nbsp;

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:14.360
At this point, Logitech&nbsp;
likely identified a problem.&nbsp;

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:18.560
Their mice were sold through PC&nbsp;
makers. If those companies or PC&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:23.960
sales struggled, so would Logitech.
Plus, the costs, were extremely high.&nbsp;

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:28.120
So, with more financial cushion,&nbsp;
the next year they launched possibly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:32.720
their most important product:
Their own retail mouse, the C7.

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:36.640
Look at that thing! Mice&nbsp;
used to look so different.&nbsp;

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:40.280
It sold for $99, or $300 today.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:44.520
A bit pricey, but it was cheaper than&nbsp;
Microsoft, and came with better software.&nbsp;

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:51.080
In its first month, it sold 800 units. Small, but&nbsp;
remember personal computers were still growing.&nbsp;

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:56.480
But this thing would start gaining momentum.
It eventually became the best-selling mouse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:01.920
of its time, even outselling Microsoft.
With this momentum, Logitech went public&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:06.240
in 1988 on the Swiss Stock Exchange,&nbsp;
then the NASDAQ 10 years later.&nbsp;

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:12.440
Logitech was growing fast, and around&nbsp;
this time, Logitech appointed a new CEO:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:19.800
Guerrino De Luca, an executive from Apple.
Everything was going great… But, how exactly?&nbsp;

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:24.080
How did they take so much ground?
What was different about Logitech?

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:29.880
Well, for one, Logitech identified,&nbsp;
ironically, they needed to detach from PCs.&nbsp;

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:36.560
They were originally sold through PC makers, and&nbsp;
switched to retail. But it goes deeper than this.&nbsp;

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:42.120
[Guerrino De Luca, CEO]: “We are in the PC&nbsp;
industry but we are not influenced by PC sales,”&nbsp;

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:45.200
[Lisa DiCarlo, Forbes, 2001]: Consider the&nbsp;
PC--sales have stagnated because there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:50.520
no compelling features driving new purchases.&nbsp;
Additionally, the starting price of about $1,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:55.680
puts it out of the range of consumers worrying&nbsp;
about the tight economy. Logitech's add-ons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.680 --> 00:04:00.600
upgrade existing PCs without breaking the bank.
[Guerrino De Luca, CEO]: “In our price range,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:06.040
consumers don't see their budgets impacted”
Might sound obvious, but Logitech was one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:09.800
of the few companies doing this.
What’s one of the easiest ways to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:13.600
make your PC experience better?
Buy a nicer mouse and keyboard.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:18.200
Even if the experience is still the&nbsp;
same, it still feels nicer to use.&nbsp;

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:23.360
Logitech also had amazing foresight.&nbsp;
Specifically for consumer preferences.&nbsp;

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:27.560
They were innovating, and trying to&nbsp;
push mice forward in all kinds of ways.&nbsp;

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:31.800
Not all of them were a hit, of course.
But some were.&nbsp;

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:37.800
Around this time, Logitech introduced the first&nbsp;
"cordless" mouse for Metaphor Computer Systems.&nbsp;

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:42.760
They also expanded into webcams and&nbsp;
other computer peripherals. In 1998,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:48.520
Logitech acquired QuickCam for $26 million.
The first webcam with a built-in microphone.&nbsp;

00:04:48.520 --> 00:04:54.840
By 2000, it had become the top seller of webcams,&nbsp;
and the following year, moved into PC speakers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:58.800
headsets, and microphones, by acquiring Labtec.
[Guerrino De Luca, CEO, 2001] "We doubled this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:03.400
company in four years and we'll do&nbsp;
the same in the next four years.”&nbsp;

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:09.760
In 2004, their revenue was $1.27&nbsp;
billion, with a nice $130 million profit.&nbsp;

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:14.600
Logitech had gone from a tiny Swiss&nbsp;
startup to a PC peripheral powerhouse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:18.040
producing its one-billionth&nbsp;
computer mouse in 2008.

00:05:18.040 --> 00:05:22.000
And this gave them an opportunity.
They had a great reputation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:27.840
in home PCs and business. But there was&nbsp;
another PC market beginning to take off.

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:42.240
In the early 2000s, the personal computer market&nbsp;
was booming. And within that market was PC gaming.&nbsp;

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:45.080
Games like World of Warcraft had just launched,&nbsp;
(Show box art on screen, Text: Nov 2004). while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.080 --> 00:05:48.040
competitive shooters like&nbsp;
Counter-Strike were taking off.&nbsp;

00:05:48.040 --> 00:05:52.640
But these didn’t use controllers.
These shooters required precise aim:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:59.400
a mouse. And World of Warcraft, required all&nbsp;
kinds of specific keys for spells and items,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:04.440
some required simultaneously.
There was a new demand for mice and keyboards.&nbsp;

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:09.560
Companies like Razer were entering the scene&nbsp;
with a focus on performance and precision.

00:06:09.560 --> 00:06:13.200
So, Logitech decided to take&nbsp;
a shot at the gaming industry.&nbsp;

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:16.960
This wasn’t their first attempt&nbsp;
at gaming, but this time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:23.560
it would be more to their strengths: Mice.
First, came the Logitech MX518 in 2005.&nbsp;

00:06:23.560 --> 00:06:26.800
If you’ve been around a while,&nbsp;
you might recognize this thing.&nbsp;

00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:30.400
Comfortable shape, but&nbsp;
extremely accurate tracking.&nbsp;

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:35.000
This thing was everywhere. It sold&nbsp;
over 16 million units in total.&nbsp;

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:39.440
The MX518 laid the foundation&nbsp;
for Logitech's iconic "G" series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.440 --> 00:06:45.840
like the G5 and G7. These mice introduced features&nbsp;
like adjustable weights and improved sensors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:50.640
all designed to give hardcore gamers more&nbsp;
precision, customization, and better performance.&nbsp;

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:56.200
Logitech had made a name for itself in&nbsp;
gaming, but it was still behind Razer.&nbsp;

00:06:56.200 --> 00:07:00.600
These mice, however, were nothing&nbsp;
compared to Logitech’s next product.&nbsp;

00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:05.480
In 2014, Logitech released the G502 Proteus Core.&nbsp;

00:07:05.480 --> 00:07:12.000
Possibly the greatest gaming mouse ever&nbsp;
made. Maybe even the best mouse ever made.&nbsp;

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:17.280
I’ve used one for years, and before&nbsp;
this mouse… was another G502.&nbsp;

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:23.720
This thing is incredible, sturdy,&nbsp;
responsive, customizable, and feels amazing.&nbsp;

00:07:23.720 --> 00:07:29.120
The G502 quickly became Logitech's signature&nbsp;
gaming mouse. Players could customize its weight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.120 --> 00:07:33.360
program extra buttons, and rely on tracking&nbsp;
that was among the best at the time.&nbsp;

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:37.440
Some people say this thing is too heavy, which&nbsp;
has spawned some pretty good memes. (Show).&nbsp;

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:40.440
But, you can just take out&nbsp;
the weights in the bottom!&nbsp;

00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:45.000
Use whatever mouse you like, but I love&nbsp;
this thing. And there’s a reason there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:51.520
are so many iterations of it. Not only that, it&nbsp;
appealed to gamers, and general desktop users,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:56.360
because it looked like a good mouse.
Razer products, as good as they are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.360 --> 00:07:59.680
do look very “gamer”.
Logitech’s gaming keyboards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:03.640
were getting similar praise too.
So, they began to expand.&nbsp;

00:08:03.640 --> 00:08:10.400
In 2017, it acquired Astro Gaming for $85 million.&nbsp;
Known for its high-end console gaming headsets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:15.800
Astro gave Logitech direct access to console&nbsp;
gamers and the growing esports scene.&nbsp;

00:08:15.800 --> 00:08:21.760
Admittedly, this one didn’t pan out so well, but&nbsp;
this was just one of many moves from Logitech.&nbsp;

00:08:22.280 --> 00:08:27.160
Gaming had become more than just playing,&nbsp;
but creating. Specifically, Streaming.&nbsp;

00:08:27.160 --> 00:08:32.720
And Logitech was eyeing this next.
In 2018, Logitech acquired Blue Microphones.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.720 --> 00:08:38.120
A lot of YouTubers, especially in the 2010s, had&nbsp;
these. The Blue Yeti, Blue Snowball Ice, it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.120 --> 00:08:43.160
squarely in the market for gaming and streaming.
Instead of relying on other companies, it could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.160 --> 00:08:49.000
now offer creators a complete setup, from mice,&nbsp;
keyboards, webcams to high-quality microphones.&nbsp;

00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:53.840
Logitech had created its own&nbsp;
ecosystem. And they went further.&nbsp;

00:08:53.840 --> 00:08:59.480
Now firmly in the streaming space, Logitech&nbsp;
acquired Streamlabs for $89 million in 2019.&nbsp;

00:08:59.480 --> 00:09:04.960
Streamlabs powers much of the streaming world.&nbsp;
70% of Twitch streamers use its software,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.960 --> 00:09:07.160
at least, according to Streamlabs.&nbsp;

00:09:07.160 --> 00:09:12.800
It’s a tool built on OBS, to manage alerts,&nbsp;
donations, overlays, and other streaming tools.

00:09:12.800 --> 00:09:16.600
Logitech had slowly secured the&nbsp;
gaming market. It wasn’t sudden.&nbsp;

00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:22.200
It gradually felt like people used less Razer,&nbsp;
and used more Logitech, as well as rising brands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.200 --> 00:09:27.120
like SteelSeries, Keychron, and Corsair.
Logitech, was firmly in the lead though.&nbsp;

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:33.680
According to a 2026 report, it holds a 19% gaming&nbsp;
mouse market share, with Razer behind at 16%.

00:09:33.680 --> 00:09:37.600
Granted, part of this was&nbsp;
Razer’s strange self-implosion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.600 --> 00:09:42.480
but a lot of credit does go to Logitech. And&nbsp;
we can see their original philosophy here.&nbsp;

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:45.960
Their gaming products felt&nbsp;
high quality, well designed.&nbsp;

00:09:45.960 --> 00:09:49.080
Not everyone can justify a giant new PC,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:54.400
but a G502 mouse? And maybe a&nbsp;
G15 keyboard? Much more likely.&nbsp;

00:09:54.400 --> 00:10:00.000
Ten years after its launch, in 2024, Logitech&nbsp;
celebrated the legendary G502 gaming mouse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:04.840
calling it the "world's most popular gaming&nbsp;
mouse," with over 21 million units sold.&nbsp;

00:10:04.840 --> 00:10:09.480
Every second, Logitech ships around&nbsp;
five products across 150 countries.&nbsp;

00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:15.560
Logitech is now number one or number two in 12 of&nbsp;
the 13 product categories in which it competes.&nbsp;

00:10:16.080 --> 00:10:21.760
But, Logitech now faces an interesting challenge.
If its hardware lasts for years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.760 --> 00:10:25.920
how does the company keep growing&nbsp;
without losing its place at the top?&nbsp;

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:30.880
Not only that, what happens when&nbsp;
leadership… starts changing priorities?&nbsp;

00:10:38.360 --> 00:10:43.000
If you’ve watched this far, consider&nbsp;
subscribing! You probably enjoy our stuff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:47.600
and it helps the channel too! Thanks.
The cracks are starting to show.&nbsp;

00:10:48.160 --> 00:10:52.960
Today, loyal customers say Logitech's&nbsp;
product quality isn't what it used to be.&nbsp;

00:10:52.960 --> 00:10:57.200
“It’s over for logitech, they stopped&nbsp;
innovation and now they want to charge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.200 --> 00:11:02.040
subscription for your mouse. And their&nbsp;
ceo thinks they are still top dog,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.040 --> 00:11:06.600
talk about being delusional and&nbsp;
disconnected from reality lol.”&nbsp;

00:11:06.600 --> 00:11:13.040
“So true. I had a G105/G402 for 7&nbsp;
years, had no issues whatsoever.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.040 --> 00:11:19.560
I bought the G915 keyboard G ProX mouse to update&nbsp;
my stuff and have wireless last December. Man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.560 --> 00:11:25.840
they disconnect almost every day. I end up using&nbsp;
the keyboard wired... WTF happened to this brand…”&nbsp;

00:11:26.560 --> 00:11:31.320
Their perception of quality is&nbsp;
slipping. Not just in hardware either.&nbsp;

00:11:31.320 --> 00:11:36.680
Maybe this is just me, but the Logitech&nbsp;
G Hub software? It is one of the worst&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.680 --> 00:11:41.480
pieces of software I’ve ever used.
It seems to need to update every day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.480 --> 00:11:46.480
it changes my G502 settings while it launches,&nbsp;
which takes forever. Meaning the mouse is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.480 --> 00:11:52.000
near-unusable until it decides it wants&nbsp;
to work. Most Logitech products are great…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:57.360
but this software is just awful.
Seems I’m not the only one either.&nbsp;

00:11:57.360 --> 00:12:01.760
I’m honestly glad they’re not a&nbsp;
software company, because yikes.&nbsp;

00:12:01.760 --> 00:12:05.720
But, speaking of that, it seems&nbsp;
their priorities may have changed.&nbsp;

00:12:05.720 --> 00:12:11.400
Recently a strange battle has been going on&nbsp;
within Logitech, over control of the company.&nbsp;

00:12:11.400 --> 00:12:16.200
Triggered by one, gigantic global event.
Logitech learned during the pandemic just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.200 --> 00:12:21.320
how lucrative the business market could be.
Its product lineup was perfectly positioned.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.320 --> 00:12:27.360
Camera, microphone, and meeting software sales&nbsp;
jumped 60% in early 2020, while webcams sold out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.360 --> 00:12:32.480
worldwide as millions of people worked from home.
Webcams, were now the winning product.&nbsp;

00:12:32.480 --> 00:12:35.640
And Logitech believes it's still&nbsp;
only scratching the surface.&nbsp;

00:12:35.640 --> 00:12:41.000
The company estimates its serviceable addressable&nbsp;
market is worth around $24 billion. Yet today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.000 --> 00:12:44.760
it generates just $4.6 billion&nbsp;
in annual revenue for Logitech.&nbsp;

00:12:44.760 --> 00:12:50.320
To close that gap, Logitech is expanding&nbsp;
into hybrid work, businesses, education,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.320 --> 00:12:55.320
healthcare, and even government, turning what&nbsp;
started as a mouse company into a much broader&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.320 --> 00:12:57.040
technology brand.
But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.040 --> 00:13:03.000
this actually came with a huge corporate battle.
After the pandemic boom, Logitech hit a slowdown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:09.320
as people stopped buying work-from-home equipment.
9 straight quarters of sales declines by 2024.&nbsp;

00:13:09.320 --> 00:13:14.640
During this time, in June 2023, CEO&nbsp;
Bracken Darrell left after a decade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.640 --> 00:13:19.360
leaving the company without a permanent leader.
Co-founder, Daniel Borel, felt the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.360 --> 00:13:23.400
current leadership, chairperson Wendy&nbsp;
Becker, wasn’t right for the company.&nbsp;

00:13:23.400 --> 00:13:29.320
For context, Borel, despite being a&nbsp;
co-founder, only held 1.5% of Logitech.&nbsp;

00:13:29.320 --> 00:13:33.520
He publicly went after her, arguing she didn't&nbsp;
have the experience to run a tech company.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.520 --> 00:13:37.240
He blamed her for failing to cut costs&nbsp;
after the pandemic and for taking too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.240 --> 00:13:41.520
long to replace Darrell.
But nobody really agreed.&nbsp;

00:13:41.520 --> 00:13:47.600
At Logitech's 2023 annual meeting, Becker&nbsp;
was re-elected with 96% of the vote.&nbsp;

00:13:47.600 --> 00:13:52.640
Borel backed off, at least for a while.
The next year, he came back.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.640 --> 00:13:57.080
Borel published an open letter to shareholders.
[Daniel Borel]: “I have proposed an alternative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.080 --> 00:14:03.600
candidate, Mr. Guy Gecht, a member of the board of&nbsp;
directors. Mr. Gecht has the necessary knowledge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.600 --> 00:14:08.600
of the high-tech industry and can provide&nbsp;
Logitech with the stability the Company needs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.600 --> 00:14:13.920
Common sense shows that he is preferable to the&nbsp;
current Chairperson, Wendy Becker, who seemingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.920 --> 00:14:18.760
has little left to contribute to Logitech as&nbsp;
it faces urgent and existential challenges.”&nbsp;

00:14:20.080 --> 00:14:25.480
Logitech tried to block Gecht being appointed,&nbsp;
but this drama had made its way to court, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:25.480 --> 00:14:30.120
ordered Gecht to be nominated for chairmanship.
But… despite the nomination,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.120 --> 00:14:35.120
he still needs to get voted in.
And he only got 14%.&nbsp;

00:14:35.120 --> 00:14:39.800
Around the same time, Logitech appointed&nbsp;
Hanneke Faber, a former Unilever executive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.800 --> 00:14:46.440
as its new CEO. Borel wasn't happy about that&nbsp;
either, arguing she also lacked a tech background.&nbsp;

00:14:47.000 --> 00:14:50.560
Logitech had become the center&nbsp;
of a public boardroom fight.&nbsp;

00:14:50.560 --> 00:14:54.960
But as you can tell, their priorities&nbsp;
were all over the place during this time.&nbsp;

00:14:55.680 --> 00:15:02.040
Even after losing, Borel eventually got what he&nbsp;
wanted. Logitech had already announced that Becker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.040 --> 00:15:08.560
wouldn't seek re-election in 2025. Then, in June&nbsp;
2025, the board nominated Guy Gecht as its next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.560 --> 00:15:13.880
chairperson, who currently holds the role.
Then came another controversy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.880 --> 00:15:20.040
In 2024, Logitech's CEO mentioned the idea of a&nbsp;
"forever mouse," one that could last for years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:20.040 --> 00:15:24.720
while users paid for software features.
Yeah people didn’t really like that idea.&nbsp;

00:15:25.320 --> 00:15:30.440
The internet quickly turned it into a&nbsp;
subscription mouse, sparking a huge backlash.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.440 --> 00:15:35.080
Logitech later clarified it was only&nbsp;
a concept, not an actual product.&nbsp;

00:15:35.080 --> 00:15:39.520
Logitech publicly clarified that there&nbsp;
were no plans for a subscription mouse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.520 --> 00:15:43.880
but it gave everyone an insight&nbsp;
into what Logitech is thinking.&nbsp;

00:15:43.880 --> 00:15:47.120
Logitech wants to be more&nbsp;
than a peripherals company.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.120 --> 00:15:50.520
It's pushing into workplace&nbsp;
technology and enterprise software,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.520 --> 00:15:55.280
chasing bigger markets but also taking&nbsp;
on tougher competition and slower sales.&nbsp;

00:15:55.280 --> 00:16:02.080
So why do this? Why annoy everyone?&nbsp;
Well, there is a reason for that.&nbsp;

00:16:02.080 --> 00:16:07.800
Logitech's biggest products all belong to&nbsp;
mature markets. Mice, keyboards, webcams,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:07.800 --> 00:16:13.040
and headsets aren't growing the way they once did.
People keep them for years, while competitors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.040 --> 00:16:18.160
keep fighting for the same customers.
Happy customers don’t give you more money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:18.160 --> 00:16:23.680
until they buy again. But that can take years.
And this is the problem.&nbsp;

00:16:23.680 --> 00:16:28.280
How do you get repeat customers&nbsp;
if you make a product that lasts?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.280 --> 00:16:33.440
Lots of companies struggle with this, and it’s&nbsp;
why some amazing companies go out of business.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.440 --> 00:16:39.520
It’s also why a lot of modern tech is designed&nbsp;
with planned obsolescence. For future revenue.&nbsp;

00:16:39.520 --> 00:16:43.920
Logitech is in a difficult spot.
They are still dominating.&nbsp;

00:16:43.920 --> 00:16:50.720
In 2026, the company kept 43.5 cents from every&nbsp;
dollar of sales before operating expenses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:50.720 --> 00:16:55.840
a sign of healthy margins. It also&nbsp;
returned $768 million to shareholders.&nbsp;

00:16:55.840 --> 00:17:00.000
But, the underlying issue is&nbsp;
that mice, keyboards, webcams,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:06.800
and headsets are all categories where replacement&nbsp;
cycles can be slow and competition is relentless.&nbsp;

00:17:06.800 --> 00:17:13.320
Yet, Logitech can’t mix things up too much, or&nbsp;
they’ll alienate or annoy their loyal customers.&nbsp;

00:17:13.320 --> 00:17:17.720
Razer figured that out the hard way.&nbsp;
Check out this video next to learn more.

